<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-inverse-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Inverse Functions</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this section, we learn how to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ol>
			<li><p>Determine if a function is one-to-one.</p></li>
			<li><p>Find the inverse of a one-to-one function.</p></li>
			<li><p>Verify that two functions are inverses of each other.</p></li>
    </ol>
	</objectives>

	<subsection>
		<title>One-to-one functions</title>

    <p>
      Recall that a function is a rule that assigns to each input exactly one output.
      Because a function is a rule, it is not possible for an input to have multiple outputs.
      Saying this same information about the graph of a function, it is not possible for the graph of a function to have two points with the same <m>x</m>-coordinate but different <m>y</m>-coordinates.
      We previously recorded the information that the graph of every function passes the <term>Vertical Line Test</term> in <xref ref="principle-vlt"/>: 
      every vertical line intersects the graph of a function in at most one point.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, because <m>f(x) = 2x</m> is a function, the graph of <m>f</m> which has equation <m>y=2x</m> passes the Vertical Line Test.
      Similarly, because <m>g(x) = x^2</m> is a function, the graph of <m>g</m> which has equation <m>y=x^2</m> passes the Vertical Line Test.
      However, the equation <m>x^2+y^2=9</m> is not the graph of a function. The equation <m>x^2+y^2=9</m> describes a circle with radius <m>3</m> centered at the origin, and the graph of this equation fails the Vertical Line Test.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this section, we focus on specific kinds of functions.
      Please keep in mind that what we have are functions in the first place.
      That is, we have a rule and by rule, we mean that each input has exactly one output.
      Said geometrically, the Vertical Line Test must pass.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under this restriction (we have a rule, or geometrically, the Vertical Line Test passes), we now consider a further restriction.
      We say that a function is <term>one-to-one</term> if <m>f(a)=f(b)</m> implies <m>a=b</m>.
      In other words, <m>a \not= b</m> implies <m>f(a) \not= f(b)</m>.
      Said differently, we say that a function is <term>one-to-one</term> if each output is the result of exactly one input.
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine if the function <m>f(x) = 2x</m> is one-to-one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Suppose we are in the situation that <m>f(a)=f(b)</m>. So <m>2a=2b</m>. Dividing both sides by <m>2</m>, we have <m>a=b</m>. 
          </p>
          <p>
            We started with <m>f(a)=f(b)</m> and showed that this implies <m>a=b</m>. Therefore, <m>f(x) = 2x</m> is a one-to-one function.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      What this means is that <m>f(x)=2x</m> has the property that each output is the result of exactly one input.
      That is, two different inputs don't share the same output for this function.
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine if the function <m>g(x) = x^2</m> is one-to-one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>g(3)=9</m> and <m>g(-3)=9</m>. So <m>g(3)=g(-3)</m> but <m>3 \not= -3</m>. So <m>g(x)</m> is not a one-to-one function.
          </p>
        </answer>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>g(4)=16</m> and <m>g(-4)=16</m>. So <m>g(4)=g(-4)</m> but <m>4 \not= -4</m>. So <m>g(x)</m> is not a one-to-one function.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      This function has many examples (we have just shown in twice) where two different inputs lead to the same output. Note that <m>g(x)</m> is a <em>function</em>, but <m>g(x)</m> is not a <em>one-to-one</em> function.
      (In other words, <m>g(x)</m> is a function, but <m>g(x)</m> isn't the special kind of function that's called a one-to-one function.)
    </p>

    <p>
      While we have already discussed that the Vertical Line Test must pass for the graph of <em>every function</em> (and note that the Vertical Line Test passes for both <m>y=2x</m> and <m>y=x^2</m>),
      notice that <m>y=2x</m> passes the Horizontal Line Test, but <m>y=x^2</m> does not.
      We introduce a geometric test for one-to-one functions:
      <principle xml:id="horizontal-line-test-iff-one-to-one">
        <title>Horizontal Line Test</title>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>f(x)</m> is a function (so its graph must pass the Vertical Line Test must pass).
          THen the function <m>f(x)</m> is one-to-one if and only if the graph of <m>f</m> passes the <term>Horizontal Line Test</term>: every horizontal line intersects the graph of <m>f</m> in at most one point.
        </p>
      </principle>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use a graphing utility to graph <m>f(x) = x^3</m> and determine if <m>f(x)</m> is one-to-one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The graph of <m>f(x) = x^3</m> passes the Horizontal Line Test, so <m>f(x)</m> is a one-to-one function.
          </p> 
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use a graphing utility to graph <m>f(x) = x^3-x</m> and determine if <m>f(x)</m> is one-to-one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The graph of <m>f(x) = x^3-x</m> fails the Horizontal Line Test, so <m>f(x)</m> is not a one-to-one function.
          </p> 
        </answer>
      </example>
      Note that <m>f(x)=x^3-x</m> is still a function, but it is not a one-to-one function.
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use a graphing utility to graph <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{x}</m> and determine if <m>f(x)</m> is one-to-one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The graph of <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{x}</m> passes the Horizontal Line Test, so <m>f(x)</m> is a one-to-one function.
          </p> 
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use a graphing utility to graph <m>f(x) = \sqrt{x}</m> and determine if <m>f(x)</m> is one-to-one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The graph of <m>f(x) = \sqrt{x}</m> passes the Horizontal Line Test, so <m>f(x)</m> is a one-to-one function.
          </p> 
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example xml:id="exponential-one-to-one">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use a graphing utility to graph <m>f(x) = 2^x</m> and determine if <m>f(x)</m> is one-to-one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The graph of <m>f(x) = 2^x</m> passes the Horizontal Line Test, so <m>f(x)</m> is a one-to-one function.
          </p> 
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Inverse functions</title>

    <p>
      The reason we look at one-to-one functions is that these functions have an inverse.
      <principle xml:id="principle-every-one-to-one-has-inverse">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Every one-to-one function <m>f(x)</m> has an inverse function <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      It is tempting to write <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> while saying or thinking <q><m>f</m> negative one <m>x</m></q>.
      However, we should either say <q><m>f</m> inverse of <m>x</m></q> or <q>inverse <m>f</m> of <m>x</m></q>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <warning>
        <p>
          Given a one-to-one function <m>f(x)</m>, its inverse is written <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> and not <m>f(x)^{-1}</m>.
        </p>
      </warning>
    </p>

    <p>
      Informally, an inverse function is a function that "undoes" the original function.
      In order to "undo" the action of a function, we have to "go backwards", so we have to switch the role of input and output, meaning we need to switch the role of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
      This leads to an overarching technique for finding the inverse of a one-to-one function.
      <principle xml:id="principle-finding-inverse">
        <title>Finding the inverse of a one-to-one function: general version</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            To find the inverse of a one-to-one function, switch <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
            (Additional work may need to be done that is specific to whether the function was introduced as a formula, a table, or a graph.)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
    </p>

    <p>
      The way that this principle plays out for a function given by formula is as follows:
      <principle xml:id="principle-finding-inverse-formula">
        <title>Finding the inverse of a one-to-one function: formula version</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            To find the inverse of a one-to-one function given by a formula:
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Replace <m>f(x)</m> with <m>y</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Switch <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> as mentioned in <xref ref="principle-finding-inverse"/>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Solve for <m>y</m> in terms of <m>x</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Replace <m>y</m> with <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the inverse of the one-to-one function <m>f(x) = 2x+3</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We start by replacing <m>f(x)</m> with <m>y</m>:
            <me>y = 2x + 3.</me>
            Next, we switch <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>:
            <me>x = 2y + 3.</me>
            Now, we solve for <m>y</m>. Subtracting <m>3</m> from both sides, we have
            <me>x - 3 = 2y.</me>
            Dividing both sides by <m>2</m>, we get
            <me>\frac{x - 3}{2} = y.</me>
            We can optionally switch the two sides of the equation:
            <me>y = \frac{x - 3}{2}.</me>
            Replace <m>y</m> with <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>:
            <me>f^{-1}(x) = \frac{x - 3}{2}.</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>


    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the inverse of the one-to-one function <m>f(x) = 4x - 5</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We start by replacing <m>f(x)</m> with <m>y</m>:
            <me>y = 4x - 5.</me>
            Next, we switch <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>:
            <me>x = 4y - 5.</me>
            Now, we solve for <m>y</m>. Adding <m>5</m> to both sides, we have
            <me>x + 5 = 4y.</me>
            Dividing both sides by <m>4</m>, we get
            <me>\frac{x + 5}{4} = y.</me>
            We can optionally switch the two sides of the equation:
            <me>y = \frac{x + 5}{4}.</me>
            Replace <m>y</m> with <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>:
            <me>f^{-1}(x) = \frac{x + 5}{4}.</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>


    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the inverse of the one-to-one function <m>f(x) = \frac{6x}{7x + 8}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            We start by replacing <m>f(x)</m> with <m>y</m>:
            <me>y = \frac{6x}{7x + 8}.</me>
            Next, we switch <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>:
            <me>x = \frac{6y}{7y + 8}.</me>
            Now, we solve for <m>y</m>.
            Multiplying both sides by <m>7y+8</m>, we get
            <me>x(7y + 8) = 6y.</me>
            Distributing <m>x</m> on the left side, we have
            <me>7xy + 8x = 6y.</me>
            Collecting all terms with <m>y</m> on one side, we get
            <me>7xy - 6y = -8x.</me>
            Factoring out <m>y</m>, we have
            <me>y(7x - 6) = -8x.</me>
            Dividing both sides by <m>7x - 6</m>, we get
            <me>y = \frac{-8x}{7x - 6}.</me>
            We can optionally switch the two sides of the equation:
            Replace <m>y</m> with <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>:
            <me>f^{-1}(x) = \frac{-8x}{7x-6}.</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
        <answer>
          <p>
            At some point in our earlier answer, we had <m>7xy + 8x = 6y</m>. As the next step, instead of getting all terms with <m>y</m> on the left side, we can move all terms with <m>y</m> to the right side:
            <me>8x = 6y - 7xy.</me>
            Factoring out <m>y</m> on the right side, we have
            <me>8x = y(6 - 7x).</me>
            Dividing both sides by <m>6 - 7x</m>, we get
            <me>\frac{8x}{6 - 7x} = y.</me>
            We can optionally switch the two sides of the equation:
            <me>y = \frac{8x}{6-7x}.</me>
            Replace <m>y</m> with <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>:
            <me>f^{-1}(x) = \frac{8x}{6-7x}.</me>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the inverse of the one-to-one function <m>f(x) = \frac{3x-4}{5x+6}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We start by replacing <m>f(x)</m> with <m>y</m>:
          <me>y = \frac{3x-4}{5x+6}.</me>
          Next, we switch <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>:
          <me>x = \frac{3y-4}{5y+6}.</me>
          Now, we solve for <m>y</m>.
          Multiplying both sides by <m>5y+6</m>, we get
          <me>x(5y + 6) = 3y - 4.</me>
          Distributing <m>x</m> on the left side, we have
          <me>5xy + 6x = 3y - 4.</me>
          Collecting all terms with <m>y</m> on one side, we get
          <me>5xy - 3y = -6x - 4.</me>
          Factoring out <m>y</m>, we have
          <me>y(5x - 3) = -6x - 4.</me>
          Dividing both sides by <m>5x - 3</m>, we get
          <me>y = \frac{-6x - 4}{5x - 3}.</me>
          Replace <m>y</m> with <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>:
          <me>f^{-1}(x) = \frac{-6x - 4}{5x-3}.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </p>

    <p>
      We can also find the inverse of a function given by a graph.
      To apply <xref ref="principle-finding-inverse"/> to a graph, if <m>(a,b)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>f</m>, then <m>(b,a)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>f^{-1}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the inverse of the one-to-one function <m>f(x)</m> whose graph is shown below.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-1,-1) grid (10,10);
                      \draw[<->] (-1,0) -- (11,0); 
                      \node at (10.5,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-1) -- (0,11);
                      \node at (.4,10.5) {$y$};
                      \draw (5,.1) -- (5,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,5) -- (-.1,5);
                      \node at (5,-.4) {5};
                      \node at (-.4,5) {5};
                      \draw (10,.1) -- (10,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,10) -- (-.1,10);
                      \node at (10,-.4) {10};
                      \node at (-.4,10) {10};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (1,5) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (2,10) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (3,2) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (4,3) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (5,9) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (6,4) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (7,6) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (8,7) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (9,1) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (10,8) circle (3pt);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>This graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> passes the Horizontal Line Test</caption>
            </figure>

          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The graph of <m>f^{-1}</m> is obtained by switching the role of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
            Since <m>(1,5)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>f</m>, <m>(5,1)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>f^{-1}</m>.
            Similarly since <m>(2,10)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>f</m>, <m>(10,2)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>f^{-1}</m>.
            Continuing in this way, the graph of <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> is
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-1,-1) grid (10,10);
                      \draw[<->] (-1,0) -- (11,0); 
                      \node at (10.5,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-1) -- (0,11);
                      \node at (.4,10.5) {$y$};
                      \draw (5,.1) -- (5,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,5) -- (-.1,5);
                      \node at (5,-.4) {5};
                      \node at (-.4,5) {5};
                      \draw (10,.1) -- (10,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,10) -- (-.1,10);
                      \node at (10,-.4) {10};
                      \node at (-.4,10) {10};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (5,1) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (10,2) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (2,3) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (3,4) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (9,5) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (4,6) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (6,7) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (7,8) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (1,9) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (8,10) circle (3pt);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>The graph of <m>y=f^[-1}(x)</m></caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      As a follow up, since <m>(5,1)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>f^{-1}</m>, then we have <m>f^{-1}(5)=1</m>.   
    </p>


    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the inverse of the one-to-one function <m>f(x)</m> whose graph is shown below.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-1,-1) grid (10,10);
                      \draw[<->] (-1,0) -- (11,0); 
                      \node at (10.5,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-1) -- (0,11);
                      \node at (.4,10.5) {$y$};
                      \draw (5,.1) -- (5,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,5) -- (-.1,5);
                      \node at (5,-.4) {5};
                      \node at (-.4,5) {5};
                      \draw (10,.1) -- (10,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,10) -- (-.1,10);
                      \node at (10,-.4) {10};
                      \node at (-.4,10) {10};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (1,4) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (2,1) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (3,7) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (4,3) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (5,8) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (6,10) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (7,2) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (8,5) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (9,6) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (10,9) circle (3pt);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>This graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> passes the Horizontal Line Test</caption>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </statement>    
        <answer>
          <p>
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-1,-1) grid (10,10);
                      \draw[<->] (-1,0) -- (11,0); 
                      \node at (10.5,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-1) -- (0,11);
                      \node at (.4,10.5) {$y$};
                      \draw (5,.1) -- (5,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,5) -- (-.1,5);
                      \node at (5,-.4) {5};
                      \node at (-.4,5) {5};
                      \draw (10,.1) -- (10,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,10) -- (-.1,10);
                      \node at (10,-.4) {10};
                      \node at (-.4,10) {10};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (4,1) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (1,2) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (7,3) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (3,4) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (8,5) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (10,6) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (2,7) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (5,8) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (6,9) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (9,10) circle (3pt);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>This graph of <m>y=f^(-1)(x)</m> is the inverse of the original function</caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      We apply this not just to scatter plots, but to any graph of a one-to-one function.
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> below, find the graph of <m>y=f^{-1}(x)</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-1,-1) grid (10,10);
                      \draw[<->] (-1,0) -- (11,0); 
                      \node at (10.5,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-1) -- (0,11);
                      \node at (.4,10.5) {$y$};
                      \draw (5,.1) -- (5,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,5) -- (-.1,5);
                      \node at (5,-.4) {5};
                      \node at (-.4,5) {5};
                      \draw (10,.1) -- (10,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,10) -- (-.1,10);
                      \node at (10,-.4) {10};
                      \node at (-.4,10) {10};
                      \draw[-, ultra thick, blue] (1,2) -- (3,5);
                      \draw[-, ultra thick, blue] (4,10) -- (9,8);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (1,2) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (3,5) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=white] (4,10) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=white] (9,8) circle (3pt);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>This graph is the graph of a one-to-one function.</caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Since the line segment connecting <m>(1,2)</m> and <m>(3,5)</m> is part of the graph of <m>f</m>, the line segment connecting <m>(2,1)</m> and <m>(5,3)</m> is part of the graph of <m>f^{-1}</m>.
            Since the line segment (with endpoints removed) connecting <m>(4,10)</m> and <m>(9,8)</m> is part of the graph of <m>f</m>, the line segment (with endpoints removed)connecting <m>(10,4)</m> and <m>(8,9)</m> is part of the graph of <m>f^{-1}</m>.
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-1,-1) grid (10,10);
                      \draw[<->] (-1,0) -- (11,0); 
                      \node at (10.5,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-1) -- (0,11);
                      \node at (.4,10.5) {$y$};
                      \draw (5,.1) -- (5,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,5) -- (-.1,5);
                      \node at (5,-.4) {5};
                      \node at (-.4,5) {5};
                      \draw (10,.1) -- (10,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,10) -- (-.1,10);
                      \node at (10,-.4) {10};
                      \node at (-.4,10) {10};
                      \draw[-, ultra thick, blue] (2,1) -- (5,3);
                      \draw[-, ultra thick, blue] (10,4) -- (8,9);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (2,1) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (5,3) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=white] (10,4) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=white] (8,9) circle (3pt);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption><m>y=f^{-1}(x)</m>.</caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      We can do this to any graph of a one-to-one function, not just scatter plots or line segments.
      Given the graph of a one-to-one function, the graph of the inverse is found by reflecting the given graph across the line <m>y=</m>.
      An effective way to do this on paper is as follows:
      take the given graph, and lightly draw in the line <m>y=x</m>. Then, for each point on the graph of the original function, 
      find the corresponding point on the other side of the line <m>y=x</m> and plot that point. Finally, connect all of these points to form the graph of the inverse function.
    </p>
    <p>
      Another way to do this is: take the given graph, and lightly draw in the line <m>y=x</m>.
      Then, rotate your paper <m>45</m> degrees clockwise, which should make the lightly drawn in line perfectly horizontal.
      Draw an upside down reflection of the roginial graph.
      Rotate the paper back to its original position, and you will have the graph of the inverse function.
    </p>


  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Properties of one-to-one functions</title>

    <p>
      <principle xml:id="principle-compose-one-to-one">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A one-to-one function <m>f(x)</m> and its inverse <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> satisfy the following:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(f^{-1}(x)) = x</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f^{-1}(f(x)) = x</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      The same information can be said about individual points:
      <principle xml:id="principle-one-to-one-points">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The point <m>(a,b)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> if and only if the point <m>(b,a)</m> is on the graph of <m>y=f^{-1}(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      Another way to phrase this is in function notation:
      <principle xml:id="principle-one-to-one-points-function">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>f(a) = b</m> if and only if <m>f^{-1}(b) = a</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
    </p>
    

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given <m>f(x) = 10x + 3</m>, and given the graph of <m>g(x)</m> below:
            <figure>
              <image width="65%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                      \draw[step=1.0, gray, very thin] (-1,-1) grid (10,10);
                      \draw[<->] (-1,0) -- (11,0); 
                      \node at (10.5,.4) {$x$};
                      \draw[<->] (0,-1) -- (0,11);
                      \node at (.4,10.5) {$y$};
                      \draw (5,.1) -- (5,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,5) -- (-.1,5);
                      \node at (5,-.4) {5};
                      \node at (-.4,5) {5};
                      \draw (10,.1) -- (10,-.1);
                      \draw (.1,10) -- (-.1,10);
                      \node at (10,-.4) {10};
                      \node at (-.4,10) {10};
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (1,4) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (2,1) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (3,7) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (4,3) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (5,8) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (6,10) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (7,2) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (8,5) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (9,6) circle (3pt);
                      \draw[ultra thick, blue, fill=blue] (10,9) circle (3pt);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>This graph of <m>y=g(x)</m> passes the Horizontal Line Test</caption>
            </figure>            
            find the following:
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(10)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>g(10)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>g^{-1}(10)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(g(6))</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(g^{-1}(6))</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(10) = 10(10) + 3 = 100 + 3 = 103</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  From the graph of <m>g</m>, we see that <m>g(10) = 9</m>.
                  We scanned for an <m>x</m>-value of <m>10</m> on the graph of <m>g</m>, and then found the corresponding <m>y</m>-value.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  From the graph of <m>g</m>, we see that <m>g^{-1}(10) = 6</m>.
                  Since we see <m>g^{-1}</m>, we scan for a <m>y</m>-value of <m>10</m> on the graph of <m>g</m>, and then find the corresponding <m>x</m>-value.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(g(6)) = f(10) = 10(10) + 3 = 100 + 3 = 103</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                  We replaced <m>g(6)</m> with <m>10</m> because the point <m>(6,10)</m> is on the graph of <m>g</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(g^{-1}(6)) = f(9) = 10(9) + 3 = 90 + 3 = 93</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                  We replaced <m>g^{-1}(6)</m> with <m>9</m> because the point <m>(9,6)</m> is on the graph of <m>g</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>

      </example>
    </p>

    <principle xml:id="principle-inverse-domain-range">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                The odomain of <m>f^{-1}</m> is the range of <m>f</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The range of <m>f^{-1}</m> is the domain of <m>f</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </principle>



  </subsection>



</section>